item.text:
**Reused Evidence**
None.

The local prior already shows recurring custodial abuse, one-sided violence, and weak accountability. The citations below focus on attribution, specific 2022 episodes, and the mix of repression plus partial restraint.

**New Evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: India","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089116.html","claim":"The report describes India as a federal parliamentary democracy but says 2022 included arbitrary killings, torture, arbitrary detention, press restrictions, internet restrictions, assembly limits, minority-targeted violence, and broad impunity.","locator":"lines 34-39","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary State Department country report summarizing 2022 with detailed rights categories and institutional context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"National environment under Modi's premiership; some abuses are state-level, but central oversight and policy context are attributable at the national level.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes civilian authorities maintained effective control over the security forces and that India remains a constitutional democracy."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: India","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089116.html","claim":"The report says there were credible reports of custodial killings, torture, forced confessions, and life-threatening prison conditions, while NHRC complaint channels existed but few prisoners used them and police often failed to comply with custodial-violence monitoring orders.","locator":"lines 43-49, 61-65, 71-79","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary State Department country report with specific custodial and prison findings tied to 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable in part to the national executive and security architecture, though many incidents are carried out by state police and local custodial actors.","contrary_evidence":["Some cases saw suspensions, arrests, or court-martial proceedings, which shows the presence of at least partial remedial action."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: India","publisher":"U.S. Department of State","publication_date":"2023-03-20","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089116.html","claim":"The report says restrictions in Jammu and Kashmir, harassment of journalists and activists, and weak compliance with Supreme Court monitoring orders remained serious problems, even as the NHRC continued prison monitoring.","locator":"lines 73-81","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary State Department country report with explicit discussion of protest, press, and detention oversight issues.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is partly national-policy relevant, but the operational conduct is mostly by union-territory and state security organs rather than Modi personally.","contrary_evidence":["The report also records that the NHRC made unannounced prison visits and that formal complaint channels existed."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: India","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/india","claim":"HRW says the BJP-led government continued systematic discrimination toward minorities, BJP supporters carried out violent attacks, and authorities used terrorism charges and foreign-funding rules to silence civil society and journalists.","locator":"lines 70-74","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major independent rights report summarizing 2022 with clear attribution and broad coverage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses 2022 conduct.","ruler_attribution":"The report frames this as government environment under BJP rule; direct PM order is not shown, but tolerance and policy environment are implicated.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes that the Supreme Court effectively halted use of sedition law in May 2022, showing some judicial restraint against executive coercion."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: India","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/india","claim":"HRW says the government reduced AFSPA coverage in some northeast districts in March 2022, but the law remained in force in Jammu and Kashmir and much of the northeast, preserving effective immunity for security personnel.","locator":"line 89","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent rights report summarizing a concrete 2022 policy change and its limits.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"A national-government decision under Modi's premiership, though the underlying security context is inherited and region-specific.","contrary_evidence":["This is a partial restraint signal, not evidence of broad reform, because the immunity structure still covered large areas."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: India","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/india","claim":"HRW says violence continued in Jammu and Kashmir, no independent investigation was made public for some killings, and the Kashmir Press Club was taken over and shut by actors aligned with the government and police.","locator":"lines 79-82, 112-115","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent rights report with specific incident coverage and attribution limits.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The conduct is not uniquely traceable to Modi personally, but it reflects a national security environment and tolerated coercion in a Union Territory under central influence.","contrary_evidence":["The same area also faced militant attacks and civil unrest, so the security context was genuinely dangerous rather than purely state-generated."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India: Persecution of minorities and shrinking space for dissent: Amnesty International: Submission to the 41st session of the UPR Working Group, November 2022","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-04-21","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/asa20/5491/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty says India had amended laws that threatened expression and peaceful assembly, discriminated against Muslims and LGBTI people, and raised concerns about religious violence, arbitrary deprivation of nationality, and broader pressure on human rights defenders.","locator":"lines 68-69","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major rights-advocacy submission focused on current legal and political conditions in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 period.","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the Modi-era legal and political environment, though it is not a direct order-level attribution document.","contrary_evidence":["It is an advocacy submission, so it is best used with official and incident-level sources rather than alone."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.7","3B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Anti-Conversion Laws and Growing Intolerance in India","publisher":"U.S. Commission on International Religious Freedom","publication_date":"2022-05-06","url":"https://www.uscirf.gov/news-room/uscirf-spotlight/anti-conversion-laws-and-growing-intolerance-india","claim":"USCIRF says the Indian government promoted Hindu nationalism and used national and state laws in ways that contributed to systematic and egregious violations of religious freedom, especially for non-Hindus.","locator":"lines 101-104","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official U.S. government commission commentary, but framed as a policy spotlight rather than a formal judicial finding.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attribution is to the national government environment under Modi, not to a specific personal incitement event.","contrary_evidence":["The format is a spotlight/podcast page, so it is less formal than the commission's annual report chapter."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.5","3B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"‘Those booked under Section 124A can approach courts for bail’: Highlights from Supreme Court order on sedition law","publisher":"The Indian Express","publication_date":"2022-05-11","url":"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/supreme-court-sedition-centre-order-7911032/","claim":"The Supreme Court said it was not appropriate to continue using the sedition law while review was pending and urged central and state governments not to register FIRs or take coercive measures under section 124A.","locator":"lines 46-56","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting on a Supreme Court interim order with direct procedural effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"This is judicial restraint on the executive rather than a ruler action, but it is relevant as a counterweight to coercive domestic security use.","contrary_evidence":["The order shows the judiciary checking coercive use of sedition, but it does not prove the executive had already stopped using the law in practice."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Information received concerning forced evictions, housing and property demolitions against Muslim minority communities in India","publisher":"OHCHR Special Procedures Communications","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://spcommreports.ohchr.org/TmSearch/Mandates?m=25","claim":"Three UN special rapporteurs said they had information on forced evictions and property demolitions directed against Muslim communities in Khargone, Anand, and Jahangirpuri, allegedly as punishment for intercommunal violence.","locator":"lines 2163-2172","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UN special-procedures communication naming places, communities, and alleged motive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The alleged acts were carried out by local authorities in BJP-governed settings, so attribution to Modi is indirect rather than a direct national order.","contrary_evidence":["This is a communications entry, not a final fact-finding judgment or court decision."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Concluding observations on the fourth periodic report of India","publisher":"UN Human Rights Committee","publication_date":"2024-09-02","url":"https://docstore.ohchr.org/SelfServices/FilesHandler.ashx?enc=53rWFWALpN2IftMZirLNzGwVjQBrMGpRrfNEKjuy%2FrtFTUdj9oE9N9HAqmtCwaLlKd2li%2FKVRdaJG4ktwy71Sg%3D%3D","claim":"The Human Rights Committee later flagged 2022 demolitions affecting religious minorities after Ram Navami riots and reports of cow-vigilante violence, indicating unresolved accountability problems from the target year.","locator":"paragraph 45","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Later treaty-body concluding observations are credible and detailed, but they are retrospective on 2022 events.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Retrospective on 2022 incidents.","ruler_attribution":"The concerns point to state and local authority failures under the Modi-era environment, but the source is retrospective and not a direct command record.","contrary_evidence":["Because it is retrospective, it is less direct than a contemporaneous incident report and should not be treated as a standalone 2022 finding."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India: Surge in Summary Punishments of Muslims","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/10/07/india-surge-summary-punishments-muslims","claim":"HRW says several BJP-ruled states used bulldozer demolitions and public flogging against Muslims after communal clashes and anti-Islam remarks, often without legal authorization or due process.","locator":"lines 609-623","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed incident report with multiple named events, dates, and contemporaneous attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The conduct is mostly state and local, but it occurred within a BJP-led political environment and was widely treated as state-tolerated punitive violence.","contrary_evidence":["Authorities often defended the demolitions as enforcement against illegal structures, so the legal justification was contested rather than conclusively disproven in every case."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India rushes to quell outrage after insulting remarks on Islam","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/06/07/us-india-politics-diplomacy","claim":"Reuters reported that after BJP officials made remarks about the Prophet, the government moved to calm anger, 38 people were arrested for rioting in Kanpur, and party discipline measures followed.","locator":"lines 637-655","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on a major domestic religious-fury episode and the government's response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The initial incitement came from BJP officials rather than Modi personally, but the episode reflects the governing party environment and the government's crisis response.","contrary_evidence":["The government and BJP later suspended or expelled some officials, which is a restraint signal even if it came after the inciting episode."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Muslim properties razed in New Delhi after communal violence","publisher":"Associated Press","publication_date":"2022-04-20","url":"https://www.news4jax.com/news/world/2022/04/20/muslim-properties-razed-in-new-delhi-after-communal-violence/","claim":"AP reported that authorities razed Muslim-owned shops in Jahangirpuri before the Supreme Court halted the demolitions, days after communal violence in the neighborhood.","locator":"lines 91-106, 113-121","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous AP reporting with photos and direct incident description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The act was carried out by local authorities and police in a BJP-governed setting, so it is indirect rather than a direct Modi order.","contrary_evidence":["Officials said the drive targeted illegal structures, so there was an asserted regulatory rationale alongside the punitive interpretation."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.9","3B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across State Department reporting, HRW, Amnesty, USCIRF, OHCHR special-procedure communications, Indian Express/AP/Reuters-syndicated reporting, and a few later treaty-body follow-ups. I kept the ten URLs above because they add distinct value on attribution, protest handling, minority targeting, detention abuse, and partial restraint.

Removed or not used:
- Duplicates or near-duplicates of the same demolitions, sedition, or protest episodes from multiple outlets.
- Out-of-period retrospectives from 2023-2026 when they added no materially new 2022 fact.
- Broad commentary pages that repeated general hostility claims without an incident, mechanism, or dated ruling.
- Sources that were too state-level for clean national attribution unless paired with a national-policy or BJP-environment signal.
- Paywalled, blocked, or cache-miss pages when the accessible snippet already gave the needed fact or when a stronger source existed.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct evidence that Modi personally ordered or approved specific police, demolition, or detention actions is still thin. Most material shows a BJP-led governing environment, state authorities, or union oversight rather than a signed PM directive.
- More search on central-government instructions to state police or home ministry coordination would help sharpen attribution.
- There is still limited public evidence on internal accountability outcomes, especially prosecutions of officers or officials after the 2022 episodes.
- If you want a second pass, the highest-value next targets are central cabinet / home ministry records, additional UN special-procedure replies, and direct 2022 local-language reporting from the affected states.
